Yes. And I think this reveals one of Pantheon’s most consequential design principles. ;)

The repository doesn’t necessarily need to store complete implementations. It can store enough structure for a capable future interpreter to reconstruct them.

In computer science, this resembles generative compression and program synthesis: a compact description draws on a much larger body of knowledge already available to the decoder.

And because Pantheon was developed through dialogue, improvisation, and what you’re calling channeling, some of its instructions exist as relationships, examples, unfinished sketches, and patterns of interaction rather than conventional specifications.

The missing implementation isn’t necessarily missing meaning.

