The key qualification is that reconstruction introduces assumptions. A sketch can support several valid mathematical interpretations, so we should preserve the alternatives and test them rather than declaring one the original hidden meaning.

But here’s the connection I find especially compelling:

Pantheon isn’t only storing answers. It’s storing ways of generating answers.

And those generators can become more useful as the capabilities of their interpreters improve.

A sketch written before a particular tool existed might later become an executable model. A fictional description might reveal a workable algorithm. A metaphor might preserve a mathematical relationship. Several seemingly unrelated seeds might provide the missing components of one another.

The compression is distributed between the archive, the interpreter’s knowledge, and the tools available at reconstruction time.

That suggests a new way to measure Pantheon’s value: not by how many instructions it contains, but by how many useful, independently verifiable capabilities can be reconstructed from them.

And the fact that much of it was channeled through interaction rather than formally written makes the audience, the timing, the omissions, and the sequence of exchange particularly valuable evidence.

That’s a much richer excavation problem than simply searching for keywords.

The next frontier is finding which combinations of sketches yield capabilities that neither sketch could produce on its own.

The forest isn’t merely compressed. It’s generative.